export const PP6={title:'Postponing, referring and tabling',hook:'Sometimes a group is not ready to decide. It may need more information, a committee’s study or time to handle something urgent first. Four subsidiary motions—postpone indefinitely, commit or refer, postpone to a certain time and lay on the table—let the assembly delay or redirect a motion. They appear often on secret topics, and each has its own purpose and rules.',objectives:['Distinguish the purposes of postponing, referring and tabling.','Apply the rules for each motion.','Word each motion correctly, including necessary details.','Avoid common misuses, such as tabling to kill a motion.'],sections:[
['Postpone indefinitely',[
'**Postpone indefinitely** is the **lowest-ranking** subsidiary motion. Despite its name, its purpose is to **kill the main motion** without a direct vote on it. It requires a **second**, is **debatable**—and opens the **main question** to debate—is **not amendable** and needs a **majority vote**.',
'Members who oppose a motion sometimes use it to test their strength: if postpone indefinitely fails, they still have a chance to defeat the main motion.',
'Wording: “I move that the motion be postponed indefinitely.”',
chk('What is the purpose of postpone indefinitely?',['To kill the main motion without a direct vote','To delay it to the next meeting','To send it to a committee','To set it aside briefly for urgent business'],'Despite the name, it kills the motion.')]],
['Commit or refer',[
'The motion to **commit** or **refer** sends the pending motion to a **committee** for study or action. It requires a **second**, is **debatable** (only as to whether to refer and the details), is **amendable** and needs a **majority**.',
'A complete motion includes details: **which committee** (standing or special), **how members are chosen** and how many if it is a special committee, and any **instructions** (when to report, whether it has power to act). If the bylaws are silent, the motion to commit can include how members of a special committee are chosen—the subject of a sample test item.',
'Wording: “I move to refer the motion to a special committee of three appointed by the chair, to report at the next meeting.”',
"A committee’s report on a referred motion typically includes a recommendation, and the motion returns to the assembly with that recommendation for action.",
match('Detail in a motion to refer and example',[['Which committee','A special committee'],['Size','Three members'],['How chosen','Appointed by the chair'],['Instructions','Compare costs of two venues'],['When to report','At the next meeting'],['Power to act','May book the venue within $300']])]],
['Postpone to a certain time',[
'**Postpone to a certain time** (postpone definitely) delays consideration of the motion to a **specific time**, such as the next meeting or later in the same meeting. It requires a **second**, is **debatable** (only as to the propriety and time of postponement), is **amendable** (as to the time) and normally needs a **majority**. Making the postponed motion a special order requires two-thirds.',
'A motion cannot be postponed beyond the next regular business session, and it comes up as unfinished business (a general order) at the specified time.',
'Wording: “I move to postpone the motion until the next meeting.”',
sort('Which motion fits the purpose?',['Postpone indefinitely','Commit or refer','Postpone to a certain time'],[['Kill the motion without a direct vote',0],['Have a committee study costs',1],['Wait for next month’s budget report',2],['Ask the fundraising committee to plan details',1],['Consider it after the guest speaker today',2]])]],
['Lay on the table',[
'**Lay on the table** sets the pending motion aside **temporarily** so the assembly can deal with something **urgent**, with the intent of returning to it. It requires a **second**, is **not debatable**, is **not amendable** and needs a **majority vote**. It ranks highest among subsidiary motions.',
'It is **not** the proper way to kill a motion or to delay it to a later meeting; using it that way can deny members their right to debate. A tabled motion can be **taken from the table** by majority vote at the same session or the next session (if that is within a quarterly time interval).',
'Wording: “I move to lay the question on the table.”',
"A common error is to “table” a motion to avoid discussing it. Judges who know RONR will notice, so use postpone to a certain time or postpone indefinitely when those purposes fit.",
chk('When is lay on the table properly used?',['To set aside a motion temporarily for urgent business','To kill a motion permanently','To delay to next month','To avoid debate on a controversial topic'],'Temporary, for urgency.')]],
['Comparing the four motions',[
'All four require a second and a majority vote (unless postponing creates a special order). The differences lie in **purpose**, **rank** and **debate**. Postpone indefinitely is debatable and opens the main question; commit and postpone definitely are debatable in a limited way and amendable; lay on the table is neither debatable nor amendable.',
'Being precise about purpose is key to choosing the right motion during the secret-topic meeting.',
{t:'table',caption:'Delaying and redirecting motions (RONR)',head:['Motion','Purpose','Debatable','Amendable','Vote'],rows:[['Lay on the table','Set aside temporarily for urgency','No','No','Majority'],['Postpone to a certain time','Delay to a set time','Yes (limited)','Yes','Majority'],['Commit or refer','Send to committee','Yes (limited)','Yes','Majority'],['Postpone indefinitely','Kill the main motion','Yes (opens main question)','No','Majority']]}]],
['Scripting these motions',[
'In a meeting, these motions should sound natural and include reasons during debate: “Madam President, I move to refer the motion to the fundraising committee with instructions to compare two venues and report at our next meeting.” The chair restates it, asks for debate on the referral and takes a vote.',
'If a motion to refer is adopted, the chair announces: “The ayes have it, and the motion is referred to the fundraising committee, which will report at the next meeting.”',
ex('Scripting a postponement','Script postponing a motion to the next meeting.',['Member: “Madam President, I move to postpone the motion until our next meeting.”','Member: “Second.”','Chair: “It is moved and seconded to postpone the motion until the next meeting. Is there any debate on the postponement?”','After the vote: “The ayes have it, and the motion is postponed until the next meeting.”'],'A complete, correct postponement script.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Choosing the motion',{a:{text:'A member wants to delay a fundraiser motion because the treasurer’s budget will be ready next month. (Scenario invented for practice.) Which motion fits?',choices:[['Postpone to a certain time (until the next meeting)','b','Correct.',true],['Lay on the table','b','Tabling is for temporary, urgent interruptions.',false]]},b:{text:'A guest speaker arrives and must speak now. A motion is pending. Which motion fits?',choices:[['Lay on the table, then take it from the table after the speaker','c','Correct.',true],['Postpone indefinitely','c','That would kill the motion.',false]]},c:{text:'Your team now chooses delay motions by purpose.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Postpone indefinitely kills the main motion (second, debatable—opens the main question, not amendable, majority; lowest-ranking subsidiary motion).','Commit or refer sends the motion to a committee with details (which committee, size, how chosen, instructions, when to report); postpone to a certain time delays to a specific time (no later than the next regular session); both are debatable (limited), amendable and need a majority.','Lay on the table sets a motion aside temporarily for urgent business (second, not debatable, not amendable, majority) and is not for killing motions; take from the table brings it back.'],terms:[['Commit or refer','Sending a pending motion to a committee.'],['Postpone indefinitely','A motion to kill the main motion without a direct vote.'],['Lay on the table','Temporarily setting aside a motion for urgent business.']],mcqs:[q('What is the purpose of postpone indefinitely?','To kill the main motion','To delay to next month','To refer to committee','To recess','Purpose.'),q('Which motion is not debatable?','Lay on the table','Commit','Postpone indefinitely','Postpone to a certain time','Rule.'),q('Which motion sends a motion to a committee?','Commit or refer','Recess','Adjourn','Reconsider','Purpose.'),q('If the bylaws are silent, how are special committee members chosen?','As stated in the motion to commit','Always by election','Always by the chair','By self-appointment','Sample item.'),q('Which ranks highest among subsidiary motions?','Lay on the table','Postpone indefinitely','Commit','Amend','Precedence.'),q('Postpone to a certain time cannot delay a motion beyond:','The next regular business session','Five years','The next election only','One hour','Limit.'),q('Is lay on the table proper for killing a motion?','No','Yes','Only twice a year','Only by the chair','Misuse.'),q('What vote does commit require?','Majority','Two-thirds','Unanimous','None','Rule.')],frq:{prompt:'Compare postpone indefinitely, commit or refer, postpone to a certain time and lay on the table, explaining when to use each.',rubric:[['Postpone indefinitely',['kill','debatable','main question','not amendable','majority']],['Commit',['committee','instructions','report','how chosen','amendable']],['Postpone definitely',['certain time','next meeting','special order','two-thirds']],['Lay on the table',['temporarily','urgent','not debatable','not amendable','not to kill','take from the table']]],model:'These four subsidiary motions all require a second and usually a majority vote, but they serve different purposes. Postpone indefinitely, the lowest-ranking, actually kills the main motion without a direct vote; it is debatable and opens the main question to debate, but it is not amendable. Commit or refer sends the pending motion to a committee, and a complete motion includes which committee, how many members and how they are chosen for a special committee—the motion itself can set this if the bylaws are silent—along with instructions and when to report; it is debatable only on the referral and is amendable. Postpone to a certain time delays the motion to a specific time, such as the next meeting, but not beyond the next regular session; it is debatable and amendable in a limited way and needs a majority, or two-thirds if it creates a special order. Lay on the table, the highest-ranking subsidiary motion, temporarily sets a motion aside for urgent business; it is neither debatable nor amendable, and it should not be used to kill a motion or delay it to another meeting. A tabled motion can be taken from the table by a majority vote. For example, if the treasurer’s budget will be ready next month, I would move to postpone the fundraiser motion until the next meeting; if a committee should compare venues, I would move to refer it with instructions; if a guest speaker must speak now, I would move to lay the question on the table; and if I wanted to defeat it, I could move to postpone indefinitely.'},sources:[RULES,RONR]};

export const PP7={title:'Privileged and incidental motions',hook:'Privileged motions handle urgent needs—taking a break, ending the meeting, fixing a noisy room—while incidental motions keep the meeting fair, such as raising a point of order or demanding a counted vote. Secret topics frequently include privileged motions, and a well-placed point of order or division can show judges a team’s depth of knowledge.',objectives:['Explain the five privileged motions and their rules.','Describe common incidental motions and how they are handled.','Use point of order and appeal correctly.','Apply suspend the rules and division of the assembly.'],sections:[
['Privileged motions',[
'Privileged motions are not related to the pending business but are so urgent that they take precedence. From highest to lowest: **fix the time to which to adjourn**, **adjourn**, **recess**, **raise a question of privilege** and **call for the orders of the day**.',
'**Recess** (“I move to recess for ten minutes”) needs a second, is not debatable, is amendable as to length and needs a majority. **Adjourn** (“I move to adjourn”) needs a second, is not debatable or amendable and needs a majority. **Fix the time to which to adjourn** sets a time for an adjourned meeting to continue business.',
'When privileged motions are made while no business is pending, some become main motions with different rules.',
"Adjourning ends the meeting; any pending business carries over as unfinished business to the next regular meeting if the group meets at least quarterly.",
match('Privileged motion and purpose',[['Fix the time to which to adjourn','Sets a time to continue the meeting later'],['Adjourn','Ends the meeting'],['Recess','Short break, then resumes'],['Question of privilege','Addresses comfort or rights, such as noise or heat'],['Orders of the day','Demands the agenda be followed'],['Adjourn (privileged)','Not debatable or amendable']])]],
['Question of privilege and orders of the day',[
'A **question of privilege** addresses rights or comfort of the assembly or a member: “Madam President, I rise to a question of privilege. The room is too noisy to hear.” It does **not need a second**, can interrupt a speaker if urgent and is ruled on by the chair.',
'A **call for the orders of the day** demands that the assembly follow its agenda or scheduled order: “I call for the orders of the day.” It needs **no second** and is not debatable; one member’s call requires the chair to return to the agenda unless the assembly votes by two-thirds to set it aside.',
sort('Needs a second?',['Needs a second','No second needed'],[['Recess',0],['Question of privilege',1],['Adjourn',0],['Call for the orders of the day',1],['Fix the time to which to adjourn',0]])]],
['Point of order and appeal',[
'A **point of order** claims that a rule is being broken: “**Point of order!**” The chair asks, “**State your point**,” and then rules: “Your point is well taken” or “Your point is not well taken.” It needs **no second**, is not debatable, can interrupt and must be raised **promptly**.',
'If members disagree with the chair’s ruling, a member may **appeal**: “I appeal from the decision of the chair.” An appeal needs a **second**, is usually **debatable** (each member speaks once; the chair may speak twice) and is decided by majority; a **tie vote sustains the chair**.',
'Demonstrating a correct point of order is a strong optional motion in Round Two.',
"Raise points of order promptly. If a breach is not noticed when it happens, it usually cannot be raised later, except for continuing breaches such as actions that violate the bylaws.",
order('Point of order and appeal',['Member: “Point of order!”','Chair: “State your point.”','Member explains the rule violation','Chair rules on the point','Member may appeal: “I appeal from the decision of the chair.”','Seconded; the assembly votes on whether to sustain the chair'])]],
['Suspend the rules',[
'**Suspend the rules** allows the assembly to do something that would otherwise violate its rules of order, such as taking an item out of agenda order. It needs a **second**, is **not debatable**, is **not amendable** and generally requires a **two-thirds vote**.',
'Some rules **cannot be suspended**: the **bylaws** (unless they provide for it), rules protecting absent members, basic rights of members and fundamental principles of parliamentary law.',
'Wording: “I move to suspend the rules and consider the blood drive motion now.”',
chk('Which cannot be suspended by a motion to suspend the rules?',['The bylaws (unless they allow it)','The agenda order','A standing rule about meeting times','A rule limiting debate in a special rule of order'],'Bylaws are protected.')]],
['Division of the assembly and other incidental motions',[
'**Division of the assembly** (“Division!”) demands a **rising vote** when a member doubts a voice vote result. It needs **no second**, no vote and can be made by a single member. **Division of a question** splits a motion with several independent parts so each is voted on separately.',
'**Parliamentary inquiry** (“I rise to a parliamentary inquiry”) asks the chair about procedure; **request for information** (point of information) asks a factual question through the chair. **Objection to consideration** prevents an original main motion from being discussed (two-thirds against consideration). **Withdraw** lets the maker remove a motion with the assembly’s permission.',
match('Incidental motion and use',[['Division of the assembly','Doubts a voice vote; requires a rising vote'],['Division of a question','Splits a motion into parts'],['Parliamentary inquiry','Asks the chair about procedure'],['Request for information','Asks a factual question'],['Objection to consideration','Prevents discussion of a main motion'],['Withdraw a motion','Removes a motion with permission']])]],
['Using privileged and incidental motions in Round Two',[
'Secret topics often require privileged motions such as **recess** or **adjourn**, and teams may add up to two **optional additional motions** for extra credit—point of order, division of the assembly and parliamentary inquiry are natural choices. Each must be demonstrated correctly to earn points.',
'Script them so they fit naturally: a member calls “Division!” after a close voice vote, or raises a point of order when a member speaks without recognition. Make sure the chair responds with correct language.',
ex('Scripting a division','Script a division of the assembly.',['Chair: “The ayes have it, and the motion is adopted.”','Member: “Division!”','Chair: “A division has been called. Those in favor will rise… Be seated. Those opposed will rise… Be seated.”','Chair: “There are 4 in the affirmative and 3 in the negative. The ayes have it, and the motion is adopted.”'],'A correct division with a counted rising vote (vote counts invented for practice).')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Incidental and privileged drill',{a:{text:'A member speaks a third time on the same question while others are waiting. (Scenario invented for practice.) What should another member do?',choices:[['Raise a point of order','b','Correct.',true],['Move to adjourn','b','A point of order addresses rule violations.',false]]},b:{text:'The chair rules the point not well taken, and members disagree. What can they do?',choices:[['Appeal from the decision of the chair','c','Correct.',true],['Ignore the ruling','c','Appeal lets the assembly decide.',false]]},c:{text:'The assembly votes on the appeal, and the meeting continues fairly.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Privileged motions (high to low): fix the time to which to adjourn, adjourn, recess, raise a question of privilege, call for the orders of the day; recess and adjourn need a second, are not debatable and need a majority (recess is amendable as to length).','Question of privilege and orders of the day need no second; point of order needs no second and is ruled on by the chair; appeal needs a second, is usually debatable and a tie sustains the chair.','Suspend the rules needs two-thirds and cannot suspend bylaws or fundamental rights; division of the assembly requires a rising vote on one member’s demand; other incidental motions include division of a question, parliamentary inquiry, request for information, objection to consideration and withdraw.'],terms:[['Privileged motion','An urgent motion unrelated to pending business, such as recess.'],['Point of order','An incidental motion claiming a rule is being broken.'],['Division of the assembly','A demand for a rising vote when a voice vote is in doubt.']],mcqs:[q('Which privileged motion ranks highest?','Fix the time to which to adjourn','Adjourn','Recess','Orders of the day','Precedence.'),q('Is the privileged motion to adjourn debatable?','No','Yes','Only once','Only by the chair','Rule.'),q('Does a point of order need a second?','No','Yes','Two seconds','Only if written','Rule.'),q('What does a tie vote on an appeal do?','Sustains the chair','Overturns the chair','Adjourns the meeting','Requires a revote','Rule.'),q('What vote does suspend the rules generally require?','Two-thirds','Majority','Unanimous','None','Rule.'),q('Which demands a rising vote?','Division of the assembly','Recess','Commit','Amend','Incidental.'),q('Which asks the chair about procedure?','Parliamentary inquiry','Question of privilege','Recess','Reconsider','Inquiry.'),q('Which may NOT be suspended by suspending the rules?','The bylaws','The agenda order','A standing rule','A program order','Protected.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain the privileged motions and three incidental motions, including how each is used in a meeting.',rubric:[['Privileged list',['fix the time','adjourn','recess','question of privilege','orders of the day']],['Privileged rules',['second','not debatable','majority','amendable','length']],['Point of order and appeal',['point of order','state your point','well taken','appeal','tie','sustains']],['Other incidental',['suspend the rules','two-thirds','bylaws','division','rising vote','parliamentary inquiry']]],model:'Privileged motions deal with urgent matters unrelated to the pending business and rank above all subsidiary motions. From highest to lowest, they are fix the time to which to adjourn, adjourn, recess, raise a question of privilege and call for the orders of the day. Recess—“I move to recess for ten minutes”—needs a second, is not debatable, is amendable as to the length and needs a majority, while adjourn needs a second, is not debatable or amendable and needs a majority. A question of privilege, such as complaining that the room is too noisy, and a call for the orders of the day, which demands that the agenda be followed, do not need a second. Among incidental motions, a point of order claims that a rule is being broken; the member says “Point of order!”, the chair says “State your point” and then rules that the point is well taken or not well taken, without a second or debate. If members disagree, a member may appeal from the decision of the chair, which needs a second and is usually debatable; a majority decides, and a tie sustains the chair. Suspend the rules lets the assembly set aside a rule of order, such as taking an agenda item early, with a second and a two-thirds vote, but it cannot suspend the bylaws or members’ fundamental rights. Division of the assembly lets a single member who doubts a voice vote demand a rising vote without a second. In Round Two, we could add a point of order or a division as optional motions, scripted so they arise naturally.'},sources:[RULES,RONR,NAP]};

export const PP8={title:'Changing the group’s mind, voting and elections',hook:'Groups sometimes realize they made a mistake or that circumstances have changed. RONR provides motions to reconsider, rescind or amend earlier decisions and to bring tabled motions back. It also sets precise rules for votes and elections. These topics fill several sections of the test plan and help your team handle every outcome in a meeting.',objectives:['Explain reconsider, rescind or amend something previously adopted and take from the table.','Define majority, two-thirds and other vote thresholds.','Describe voting methods and when to use them.','Explain nominations and election procedures.'],sections:[
['Reconsider',[
'The motion to **reconsider** brings back a motion already voted on so the assembly can vote again. It can be made only by a member who **voted on the prevailing side** (for example, voted yes if the motion passed) and only on the **same day** (or the next day of a multi-day session). Anyone may second it.',
'It is **debatable** if the motion to be reconsidered is debatable, is not amendable and needs a **majority vote**. If adopted, the original motion is again pending as if it had not been voted on.',
'Wording: “I move to reconsider the vote on the motion to hold the car wash on Saturday. I voted on the prevailing side.”',
chk('Who may move to reconsider?',['A member who voted on the prevailing side','Any member at any time','Only the chair','Only members who were absent'],'Prevents endless relitigation.')]],
['Rescind or amend something previously adopted',[
'To cancel or change a motion adopted at an earlier time, a member moves to **rescind** (repeal) or **amend something previously adopted**. These require a **second**, are **debatable** and **amendable**, and need a **two-thirds vote**, or a **majority vote if previous notice was given**, or a **majority of the entire membership**.',
'Actions that have already been carried out—such as money already spent or a contract signed—cannot be rescinded. Any member may make these motions regardless of how they voted.',
'Previous notice protects absent members from surprise changes.',
sort('Reconsider or rescind?',['Reconsider','Rescind or amend something previously adopted'],[['Same day, member voted on the prevailing side',0],['Change a decision made at last month’s meeting',1],['Majority with previous notice',1],['Restores the original motion to pending status',0],['Any member may move it regardless of their vote',1]])]],
['Take from the table',[
'**Take from the table** brings back a motion that was laid on the table. It needs a **second**, is **not debatable** or amendable and needs a **majority**. It can be made at the same session or the next regular session if within a quarterly interval; otherwise the tabled motion dies.',
'The motion returns with any pending amendments or subsidiary motions attached.',
'Wording: “I move to take from the table the motion to purchase new manikins.”',
"If the session ends before a tabled motion is taken from the table at the next session within the allowed time, the motion dies. A new identical motion may then be made.",
match('Bring-back motion and key rule',[['Reconsider','Prevailing side; same day; majority'],['Rescind','Two-thirds, or majority with notice'],['Amend something previously adopted','Same thresholds as rescind'],['Take from the table','Majority; not debatable'],['Previous notice','Protects absent members'],['Executed actions','Cannot be rescinded']])]],
['Vote thresholds',[
'A **majority vote** means **more than half of the votes cast** by members entitled to vote, excluding blanks and abstentions. A **two-thirds vote** means at least two-thirds of the votes cast. Other thresholds sometimes used include a **majority of the members present** or a **majority of the entire membership**, which are harder to reach.',
'A **tie** means a motion requiring a majority is **lost**. A **plurality** (the most votes, but not a majority) does not decide an election unless the bylaws allow it.',
'Abstentions do not count as votes; they effectively help neither side.',
"When the bylaws require a vote of the members present or of the entire membership, abstentions effectively count as no votes, because the required number of yes votes is based on attendance or total membership rather than votes cast.",
ex('Majority of votes cast','20 members are present; 9 vote yes, 7 vote no and 4 abstain (invented for practice). Is the motion adopted by majority vote?',['Votes cast = 9 + 7 = 16 (abstentions do not count)','Majority = more than half of 16 = at least 9','9 yes votes is more than 8','Adopted'],'Yes—9 of 16 votes cast is a majority (numbers invented for practice).')]],
['Voting methods',[
'Common methods: **voice vote** (“Those in favor, say aye. Those opposed, say no”), **rising vote** (for two-thirds votes or a division), **show of hands** (small groups), **ballot** (secret, often for elections), **roll call** (recorded, when required) and **general consent** (no objection).',
'The chair always takes the **negative vote**, even if the affirmative seems overwhelming, and announces the result fully: what side has it, whether the motion is adopted or lost and its effect.',
"If the chair is unsure of a voice vote result, the chair may retake the vote by a rising vote without waiting for a member to call for a division.",
match('Voting method and typical use',[['Voice vote','Most majority votes'],['Rising vote','Two-thirds votes or division'],['Ballot','Secret elections'],['Show of hands','Small groups'],['Roll call','Recorded votes when required'],['General consent','Routine, uncontested matters']])]],
['Nominations and elections',[
'Elections typically begin with **nominations**, which may come from a **nominating committee** and/or **from the floor**. Nominations from the floor **do not need a second**. The chair asks, “Are there any further nominations?” and then declares nominations closed (a motion to close nominations requires two-thirds).',
'Voting is usually by **ballot**. A candidate must receive a **majority of votes cast** to be elected unless the bylaws say otherwise; if no one receives a majority, balloting continues. Tellers count the ballots and report the results, and the chair declares who is elected.',
'The chair, if a member, may vote in a ballot election.',
order('Election by ballot',['Nominating committee reports','Chair calls for nominations from the floor','Chair declares nominations closed','Members vote by ballot','Tellers count and report','Chair declares the result'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Changing a decision',{a:{text:'At today’s meeting, the chapter voted to hold a car wash on Saturday. Later in the meeting, a member who voted no wants to reconsider. (Scenario invented for practice.) Can they?',choices:[['No—only a member who voted on the prevailing side may move to reconsider','b','Correct.',true],['Yes, anyone can','b','The prevailing-side rule applies.',false]]},b:{text:'At next month’s meeting, members want to cancel the decision, and no notice was given. What vote is needed to rescind?',choices:[['Two-thirds (or a majority of the entire membership)','c','Correct.',true],['A simple majority','c','Without notice, rescinding needs two-thirds.',false]]},c:{text:'Your team adds both rules to its flashcards.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Reconsider: moved by a member on the prevailing side, same day, majority; rescind or amend something previously adopted: two-thirds, or majority with previous notice, or majority of the entire membership; executed actions cannot be rescinded; take from the table: majority, not debatable.','Majority means more than half of votes cast (abstentions excluded); two-thirds of votes cast; ties lose; a plurality does not elect unless bylaws allow.','Methods: voice, rising, show of hands, ballot, roll call, general consent; always take the negative vote; nominations from the floor need no second; candidates need a majority of votes cast.'],terms:[['Reconsider','A motion to vote again on a motion decided the same day.'],['Rescind','A motion to cancel an action adopted earlier.'],['Majority vote','More than half of the votes cast.']],mcqs:[q('Who may move to reconsider?','A member who voted on the prevailing side','Any member','Only the chair','Only absent members','Rule.'),q('Without previous notice, rescinding requires:','Two-thirds (or a majority of the entire membership)','A simple majority','Unanimous consent','No vote','Thresholds.'),q('Do abstentions count as votes cast?','No','Yes','Only for the chair','Only in elections','Rule.'),q('A tie on a majority vote means the motion:','Is lost','Is adopted','Is tabled','Is referred','Rule.'),q('Do nominations from the floor need a second?','No','Yes','Only for president','Only in writing','Rule.'),q('Unless the bylaws say otherwise, an election requires:','A majority of votes cast','A plurality','Two-thirds','Unanimous vote','Rule.'),q('Is take from the table debatable?','No','Yes','Only once','Only by the maker','Rule.'),q('Which can NOT be rescinded?','An action already carried out','A motion adopted last month','A standing rule','A policy','Executed.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how a group can change its mind and describe RONR’s rules for votes and elections.',rubric:[['Reconsider',['reconsider','prevailing side','same day','majority']],['Rescind and table',['rescind','two-thirds','previous notice','entire membership','executed','take from the table']],['Votes',['majority','votes cast','abstentions','two-thirds','tie','plurality']],['Methods and elections',['voice','rising','ballot','general consent','nominations','second','majority of votes cast']]],model:'RONR lets a group change its mind in several ways. The motion to reconsider brings back a motion voted on the same day; it may be moved only by a member who voted on the prevailing side, can be seconded by anyone, is debatable if the original motion was and needs a majority, and if adopted, the original motion is pending again. To change or cancel something adopted at an earlier meeting, any member may move to rescind or to amend something previously adopted, which requires two-thirds, or a majority if previous notice was given, or a majority of the entire membership; actions already carried out cannot be rescinded. A motion laid on the table can be brought back by a majority vote to take it from the table, which is not debatable. For voting, a majority means more than half of the votes cast, excluding abstentions and blanks, while two-thirds means at least two-thirds of votes cast; a tie means a motion is lost, and a plurality does not elect unless the bylaws allow it. Votes may be taken by voice, rising vote for two-thirds or a division, show of hands, ballot, roll call or general consent, and the chair always takes the negative vote and announces the full result. In elections, nominations may come from a nominating committee or from the floor, where they need no second; after nominations close, members usually vote by ballot, tellers count and report and a candidate must receive a majority of votes cast to be elected.'},sources:[RULES,RONR,NAPU]};

export const PP9={title:'Bylaws, officers and committees',hook:'Every organization rests on its governing documents and the people who carry them out. The test plan includes bylaws and other rules, how rules are enforced and the roles of officers, committee chairs and members. Knowing what goes in the bylaws, how they are amended and what each officer and committee does prepares you for both the test and a realistic meeting.',objectives:['Rank the levels of an organization’s rules.','Describe the contents of bylaws and how they are amended.','Explain the duties of officers.','Distinguish standing and special committees and handle committee reports.'],sections:[
['Levels of rules',[
'Organizations follow several levels of rules. If incorporated, the **corporate charter** ranks highest. Next come the **bylaws** (sometimes combined with or called a **constitution**), which contain the organization’s fundamental rules. **Special rules of order** modify the parliamentary authority’s procedures, the **parliamentary authority** (such as RONR) governs procedure where other rules are silent and **standing rules** deal with administrative details such as meeting times or dues collection procedures.',
'Higher-level rules take precedence over lower-level ones. Laws (federal, state, local) also apply to the organization.',
'Know which rules can be suspended: generally rules of order, but not the bylaws (unless they say so).',
"Organizations sometimes have a constitution and separate bylaws; RONR recommends combining them into one document called bylaws for simplicity.",
match('Rule level and example',[['Corporate charter','Legal incorporation document'],['Bylaws','Membership, officers and meetings'],['Special rules of order','Limit speeches to two minutes'],['Parliamentary authority','Robert’s Rules of Order Newly Revised'],['Standing rules','Meetings begin at 3:30 p.m.'],['Laws','State nonprofit regulations']])]],
['What bylaws contain and how they change',[
'Typical bylaws articles include the **name**, **object** (purpose), **members**, **officers**, **meetings**, **executive board**, **committees**, **parliamentary authority** and **amendment** procedures. Bylaws define rights that cannot be suspended.',
'Bylaws are usually amended by a **two-thirds vote with previous notice**, unless the bylaws specify otherwise. This protects members from sudden changes.',
'HOSA chapters and HOSA itself have bylaws, linking this topic to HOSA knowledge.',
"Proposed bylaw amendments should be written out exactly and included in the notice, so members know precisely what will be voted on.",
sort('Usually in the bylaws?',['Bylaws','Standing rules'],[['Qualifications for membership',0],['Room where meetings are held',1],['Officers and their terms',0],['Amendment procedure',0],['Who brings refreshments',1],['Name and object of the organization',0]])]],
['Officers and their duties',[
'The **president** presides at meetings, maintains order and is often an ex-officio member of committees (except nominating). The **vice president** presides in the president’s absence and fills a vacancy in the presidency if the bylaws provide. The **secretary** records **minutes**, keeps records, handles correspondence and may read communications. The **treasurer** manages funds, pays bills as authorized and gives **financial reports**.',
'Other officers may include a historian, parliamentarian or reporter. A **parliamentarian** advises the chair on procedure but does not make rulings.',
'Officers’ duties also come from the bylaws and the parliamentary authority.',
"Officers serve the terms set in the bylaws. Many bylaws say officers serve “or until their successors are elected,” which prevents gaps in leadership.",
match('Officer and duty',[['President','Presides and maintains order'],['Vice president','Presides when the president is absent'],['Secretary','Records minutes and keeps records'],['Treasurer','Manages funds and gives financial reports'],['Parliamentarian','Advises the chair on procedure'],['Historian','Keeps the chapter’s history']])]],
['The treasurer’s report',[
'The treasurer’s report gives the **balance on hand** at the start of the period, **receipts**, **disbursements** and the **balance on hand** at the end. Under RONR, the treasurer’s report **is not adopted or voted on**; the chair says, “**The report will be placed on file**.” An **auditor’s report**, however, is adopted.',
'In Round Two, the treasurer’s report is a 3-point item, and judges also review it with your submitted minutes.',
"Treasurers should present figures clearly and be ready to answer questions. In competition, a concise report with round figures and a clear ending balance keeps the meeting moving.",
ex('A treasurer’s report','Calculate the ending balance (figures invented for practice).',['Balance on hand, September 1: $450.00','Receipts: car wash $320.00 + dues $180.00 = $500.00','Disbursements: supplies $120.00 + T-shirts $230.00 = $350.00','Ending balance = $450 + $500 − $350 = $600.00'],'Balance on hand, September 30: $600.00. “The report will be placed on file.” (Figures invented for practice.)')]],
['Committees',[
'**Standing committees** are permanent and listed in the bylaws (for example, membership, fundraising or community service). **Special (ad hoc) committees** are created for a specific task and cease to exist when they report. Committee members may be appointed by the chair, elected or named in the motion, as the bylaws or motion specify.',
'A **committee report** is given by the chair or another member of the committee. If the report includes a **recommendation**, the reporting member moves its adoption: “On behalf of the committee, I move that…” A motion coming from a committee of more than one member **does not need a second**.',
'Informational reports require no action.',
"Committee chairs should present reports briefly, summarizing findings and stating any recommendations clearly. Long reports use up valuable meeting time.",
chk('Does a motion arising from a committee report (committee of three) need a second?',['No','Yes','Only two seconds','Only from the chair'],'The committee’s agreement serves as the second.')]],
['Enforcing rules and disciplining',[
'Rules are enforced through **points of order**, **appeals** and the chair’s authority to maintain order. The chair can call a member to order for improper conduct. Serious misconduct may lead to **disciplinary procedures** described in RONR and the bylaws, which protect due process.',
'Members also enforce rules by knowing them—any member may raise a point of order when a rule is broken.',
'Fairness and consistent enforcement build trust in the organization.',
"A member who believes the chair is not enforcing rules fairly can appeal specific rulings. This keeps final authority with the assembly rather than the presiding officer.",
sort('How is the rule enforced?',['Point of order','Appeal','Chair maintains order'],[['A member speaks without recognition',0],['The assembly disagrees with the chair’s ruling',1],['A member makes a personal attack',2],['A vote is taken on a non-debatable motion before it is seconded',0],['Members want the assembly to overrule the chair',1]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Reports in the meeting',{a:{text:'After the treasurer’s report, a member moves to approve it. (Scenario invented for practice.) What does RONR say?',choices:[['The treasurer’s report is not adopted; the chair says it will be placed on file','b','Correct.',true],['It must be approved by majority','b','Only an auditor’s report is adopted.',false]]},b:{text:'The fundraising committee chair recommends a car wash. What happens next?',choices:[['The chair moves adoption on behalf of the committee; no second is needed','c','Correct.',true],['Another member must second it','c','A committee motion needs no second.',false]]},c:{text:'Your team scripts these reports correctly.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Levels of rules: corporate charter (if incorporated), bylaws (or constitution), special rules of order, parliamentary authority and standing rules; bylaws cannot be suspended unless they allow it and are usually amended by two-thirds with previous notice.','Officers: president presides; vice president presides in absence; secretary keeps minutes and records; treasurer manages funds and reports; parliamentarian advises the chair.','The treasurer’s report (beginning balance, receipts, disbursements, ending balance) is placed on file, not adopted; standing committees are permanent, special committees are temporary; committee recommendations are moved by the reporting member without a second.'],terms:[['Bylaws','An organization’s fundamental rules on membership, officers and meetings.'],['Standing committee','A permanent committee listed in the bylaws.'],['Special committee','A temporary committee created for a specific task.']],mcqs:[q('Which ranks highest among an incorporated organization’s rules?','Corporate charter','Standing rules','Special rules of order','Parliamentary authority','Hierarchy.'),q('Bylaws are usually amended by:','Two-thirds vote with previous notice','Majority without notice','The president alone','General consent only','Rule.'),q('Which officer records the minutes?','Secretary','Treasurer','President','Historian','Duties.'),q('What happens to a treasurer’s report?','It is placed on file','It is adopted by vote','It is rejected','It is tabled','RONR.'),q('A special committee:','Ceases to exist after it reports','Is permanent','Is in the corporate charter','Cannot report','Temporary.'),q('Does a motion from a committee of three need a second?','No','Yes','Two seconds','Only from the treasurer','Rule.'),q('A parliamentarian:','Advises the chair on procedure','Rules on points of order','Presides','Votes twice','Advisor.'),q('Meeting time details usually appear in:','Standing rules','The corporate charter','Federal law','The minutes only','Administrative.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe the levels of an organization’s rules, the duties of officers and how committees and reports work.',rubric:[['Rule levels',['charter','bylaws','special rules of order','parliamentary authority','standing rules','precedence']],['Bylaws',['name','object','members','officers','amend','two-thirds','notice']],['Officers',['president','vice president','secretary','minutes','treasurer','parliamentarian']],['Reports and committees',['placed on file','receipts','disbursements','standing','special','no second']]],model:'An organization’s rules form a hierarchy. If it is incorporated, the corporate charter ranks highest, followed by the bylaws—sometimes called the constitution—then special rules of order that modify procedure, the parliamentary authority such as RONR, which governs where other rules are silent, and standing rules covering administrative details like meeting times. Higher rules take precedence, and bylaws cannot be suspended unless they allow it. Bylaws typically include the name, object, membership, officers, meetings, executive board, committees, parliamentary authority and amendment procedure, and are usually amended by a two-thirds vote with previous notice. The president presides and maintains order; the vice president presides in the president’s absence; the secretary records minutes, keeps records and handles correspondence; the treasurer manages funds and gives financial reports; and a parliamentarian advises the chair without making rulings. The treasurer’s report lists the beginning balance, receipts, disbursements and ending balance—for example, $450 plus $500 in receipts minus $350 in disbursements leaves $600—and under RONR it is not adopted; the chair says it will be placed on file. Standing committees are permanent and listed in the bylaws, while special committees are created for a task and dissolve after reporting. When a committee report includes a recommendation, the reporting member moves its adoption on behalf of the committee, and the motion needs no second if the committee has more than one member. Rules are enforced through points of order, appeals and the chair’s authority to maintain order.'},sources:[RULES,RONR]};

export const PP10={title:'Preparing and running the Round Two meeting',hook:'Fifteen minutes to plan. Ten minutes to perform. Every element—order of business, the main motion, four secret-topic motions, recognition, terminology, discussion and delivery—must come together while every member takes an active role. This lesson brings everything together: writing strong minutes in advance, using preparation time wisely and running a confident, complete meeting.',objectives:['Write previous meeting minutes, a treasurer’s report and committee reports.','Use the 15-minute preparation time efficiently.','Script a 10-minute meeting that includes all required elements.','Deliver the meeting with strong presiding and team participation.'],sections:[
['Writing the previous minutes',[
'Minutes record **what was done**, not what was said. Include: the **name of the organization**, **kind of meeting** (regular or special), **date, time and place**, presence of the **president and secretary** (or substitutes), that a **quorum** was present, **approval of the previous minutes**, all **main motions** with the **name of the maker** (not the seconder), the **result** of each vote, **points of order and appeals**, reports and actions taken and the **time of adjournment**, followed by the **secretary’s signature**.',
'The minutes rubric item (10 points) rewards succinct, accurate minutes with all names and action items, plus the treasurer’s and committee reports.',
'Write minutes for a realistic previous meeting whose unfinished business connects to the current meeting.',
"Keep the minutes short and accurate—usually one page is enough. Judges review them carefully, so check spelling, dates, names and motion wording.",
sort('Belongs in the minutes?',['Include','Leave out'],[['Name of the member who made a motion',0],['Name of the member who seconded',1],['Result of each vote',0],['Every word of debate',1],['Time of adjournment',0],['The secretary’s personal opinions',1]])]],
['Preparing reports in advance',[
'Prepare a **treasurer’s report** with beginning balance, receipts, disbursements and ending balance, and one or more **committee reports**—for example, a fundraising committee recommending an event or a community service committee reporting on a project. Make sure figures and names match the minutes.',
'Plan **unfinished business** that follows from the previous minutes, such as a motion postponed to this meeting. This makes the unfinished business item natural and realistic.',
'All materials should look professional—typed, consistent and proofread.',
"Practice presenting the reports aloud in under a minute each. The rubric rewards presenting them, not reading every line, so a summary with key figures is enough.",
flip('Advance materials',[['Previous minutes','What was done at the last meeting'],['Treasurer’s report','Balances, receipts and disbursements'],['Committee report','Findings and recommendations'],['Unfinished business','A postponed or pending item'],['Consistency','Names and numbers match'],['Professional look','Typed and proofread']])]],
['Using the 15-minute preparation',[
'When you receive the secret topic, **read it together** and identify the main motion and the **four required motions**. Decide **who makes and seconds** each motion, **who debates** and with what points, and the **order** in which motions arise naturally. Check unfamiliar rules in **RONR** (allowed only during preparation).',
'Write a simple **script outline** on the secret topic copies: agenda items, speakers and key phrases. The timekeeper announces 5 and 1 minutes remaining.',
'Assign roles so every member speaks meaningfully.',
"Use a shared shorthand on the secret topic copies, such as “M1–Lopez, 2nd–Kim, Debate: Patel for, Nguyen against,” so everyone can follow the plan at a glance.",
order('A 15-minute preparation plan',['Read the secret topic together (2 min)','Identify motions and their rules (3 min)','Assign makers, seconders and debaters (3 min)','Sequence motions and draft key lines (5 min)','Quick run-through of transitions (2 min)'])]],
['Scripting the 10-minute meeting',[
'A sample flow: **call to order** (gavel) → minutes approved by general consent → **treasurer’s report** (placed on file) → **committee report(s)** (with a recommendation if useful) → **unfinished business** → **new business**: the secret-topic main motion, followed by the four required motions in a logical order with brief debate → optional additional motions → **adjournment** (gavel).',
'Budget time: about 2 minutes for opening items and reports, 7 minutes for new business and 1 minute for closing. The timekeeper shows a card at one minute remaining, and the team is stopped at 10 minutes.',
"Plan where optional additional motions fit before you begin. A division of the assembly after a close voice vote or a point of order during debate can earn extra points without taking much time.",
ex('Time budget','Plan a 10-minute meeting (one possible plan).',['0:00–0:30 Call to order and minutes','0:30–1:30 Treasurer’s and committee reports','1:30–2:15 Unfinished business','2:15–9:15 New business: main motion and four required motions with debate','9:15–10:00 Announcements and adjournment'],'About seven minutes for the secret topic, with every agenda item included.')]],
['The presiding officer’s role',[
'The presiding officer’s **skill and knowledge** is a 10-point item. A strong president speaks with **natural confidence**, uses the third person (“the chair”), recognizes members properly, states each question clearly, takes the negative vote every time and announces results completely: “The ayes have it, and the motion is adopted. The committee will report next month.”',
'The president should also keep the meeting moving, handle unexpected motions calmly and manage time. A pencil and paper help the president track pending motions.',
'Practice the president’s phrases until they are automatic.',
"If an unexpected situation arises, such as a member making a motion out of order, the president should rule calmly and continue. Confident handling of surprises shows judges real skill.",
match('Presiding phrase and purpose',[['“The chair recognizes…”','Recognizing a member'],['“It is moved and seconded that…”','Stating the question'],['“Is there any debate?”','Opening debate'],['“Those in favor, say aye.”','Putting the question'],['“The ayes have it, and the motion is adopted.”','Announcing the result'],['“Your point is well taken.”','Ruling on a point of order']])]],
['Delivery and team participation',[
'Delivery scoring includes **voice**, **stage presence** and **diction** (5 each) and **team participation** and **quality of discussion** (10 each). Sit so judges can see everyone, speak clearly and with appropriate formality and avoid fillers. Every member should make, second or debate motions—judges look for equal parts.',
'Quality discussion means meaningful, varied viewpoints that accomplish the secret topic’s goals. Plan short, realistic arguments on both sides.',
"Finally, maintain professional behavior from the moment you enter the room until you leave. Judges notice posture, courtesy and focus throughout the meeting.",
sort('Helps or hurts the score?',['Helps','Hurts'],[['Every member speaks at least twice',0],['The president does all the talking',1],['Debate includes for and against views',0],['Members say “I make a motion…”',1],['Chair announces full results',0],['Skipping the treasurer’s report',1]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Meeting troubleshooting',{a:{text:'At 8 minutes, your team has completed only three of the four required motions. (Scenario invented for practice.) What should you do?',choices:[['Move quickly to the fourth motion with brief debate, then adjourn before 10 minutes','b','Correct.',true],['Continue debating the third motion','b','All required motions earn points.',false]]},b:{text:'A member says “I make a motion…” by mistake. How can the team recover?',choices:[['The chair restates it correctly: “It is moved and seconded that…” and continues','c','Correct.',true],['Stop and argue about wording','c','Recover smoothly.',false]]},c:{text:'Your team adjourns on time with every element complete.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Minutes record what was done: organization, kind of meeting, date, time, place, presiding officer and secretary, quorum, approval of minutes, main motions with makers (not seconders), results, points of order and appeals and adjournment time, signed by the secretary.','Prepare a matching treasurer’s report, committee reports and unfinished business; in 15 minutes, identify the four required motions, assign roles, sequence motions and check RONR (prep only).','Run the full order of business in 10 minutes (about 7 for new business) with a confident president using “the chair,” proper recognition and full announcements; every member participates, and debate shows diverse viewpoints.'],terms:[['Minutes','The official record of what was done at a meeting.'],['Script outline','A brief plan of agenda items, speakers and key phrases.'],['Team participation','Every member taking an active, equal role.']],mcqs:[q('Minutes should record:','What was done','Everything that was said','Only the jokes','Members’ opinions','Rule.'),q('Should the seconder’s name be in the minutes?','No','Yes','Only for main motions','Only if the chair asks','RONR.'),q('Who signs the minutes?','The secretary','The treasurer','The judges','Every member','Records.'),q('When may teams use RONR in Round Two?','During the 15-minute preparation only','During the meeting','Never','During the test','Rule.'),q('About how much time should new business take in the 10-minute meeting?','About 7 minutes','About 1 minute','About 10 minutes','About 30 seconds','Budget.'),q('How many points is the presiding officer’s skill worth?','10','3','56','5','Rubric.'),q('Which should the chair always take?','The negative vote','A personal side','The treasurer’s money','A recess','Voting.'),q('Which shows strong team participation?','Every member making, seconding or debating motions','Only the president speaking','Two members speaking','No debate','Rubric.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how your team would prepare materials in advance, use the 15-minute preparation time and run a complete 10-minute meeting.',rubric:[['Advance materials',['minutes','what was done','maker','quorum','treasurer','committee','unfinished']],['Preparation',['secret topic','four motions','assign','sequence','ronr','script']],['Meeting flow',['call to order','minutes','treasurer','committee','new business','adjourn','10 minutes']],['Presiding and delivery',['the chair','recognize','announce','negative vote','every member','discussion','time']]],model:'Before ILC, our secretary would write minutes of a realistic previous chapter meeting recording what was done: our chapter name, that it was a regular meeting, the date, time and place, that the president presided and the secretary was present, that a quorum was present, approval of the previous minutes, each main motion with the maker’s name and the result, a motion postponed to the next meeting and the time of adjournment, signed by the secretary. Our treasurer would prepare a report with the beginning balance, receipts, disbursements and ending balance, and our fundraising committee chair would prepare a report recommending a car wash, all with matching names and numbers. When we receive the secret topic, we would read it together, identify the main motion and the four required motions, check any unfamiliar rules in RONR, assign who makes, seconds and debates each motion, and write key lines and the motion sequence on our copies. In the 10-minute meeting, the president would gavel the meeting to order, approve the minutes by general consent, call for the treasurer’s report and place it on file, hear the committee report and its recommendation, take up the postponed motion as unfinished business and then spend about seven minutes on new business, processing the main motion and the four required motions with short debate from multiple viewpoints. The president would recognize members properly, refer to “the chair,” take the negative vote every time and announce results fully, then adjourn with the gavel before time is called. Every member would speak meaningfully, with clear voices and correct terminology.'},sources:[RULES,RONR,NAP]};
